{"purpose":"Capture the intent behind two CLI experience enhancements for eka-cli: (1) `eka init` installs the EKA standard declaration file into the generated repository, sourced from the eka-standard GitHub release asset and embedded in the CLI binary; (2) root help output groups the command list by intent and applies the CLI color palette (command names in primary/Info, flag names in Dim).","content":"## 1. eka init installs the EKA standard declaration\n\nThe generated repository currently contains eka.yaml only (ADR-020, identity-only). `eka init` should also write the EKA standard declaration file — the root `EKA` file (compact consumer summary with a `Version X.Y` line) published by the eka-standard repository (github.com/maleolabs/eka-standard) as its single GitHub release asset (contract: tag scheme vMAJOR.MINOR, version consistency gate in .github/workflows/release.yml, fetchable at releases/latest/download/EKA or releases/download/v1.0/EKA; asset verified live on 2026-08-14).\n\nDecided mechanics (planning discussion 2026-08-14, evidence cmt:cli-experience-discussion): the file is VENDORED from the release asset into the CLI binary (embed, ADR-023 pattern) and copied by `eka init` — init stays offline, dry-run stays deterministic, and the version is single-sourced. Runtime fetching at init is rejected: it would regress the offline init property, break dry-run determinism, and create a conformance lie (a repository declaring a standard newer than the rules compiled into the validating CLI).\n\nConsequences: ADR-020 Decision 1 needs an amendment (carve-out: identity-only + standard declaration file); `standardVersion` (cmd/version.go, currently a hardcoded const) is derived from the embedded file, resolving the standard-corpus axis of sto:version-clarity.\n\n## 2. Help output grouped by intent and colored\n\nThe root help (`Available Commands`) and the landing `Commands` list are grouped by intent — Authoring / Repository \u0026 Exchange / Knowledge Access / Runtime \u0026 Workspace / Utility — using cobra command groups as the data model and a shared renderer. The five Engineering Domains are rejected as grouping labels: they describe knowledge artifacts, not CLI operations (Discovery/Architecture/Planning have no pure commands; the result would be a large catch-all).\n\nColors, TTY-only: command names in Info (soft blue, the palette's primary/heading hue), flag names in Dim (gray, secondary tier), group headers in Accent, the help hint in Dim, descriptions plain. Amber (Warning) is rejected for flags: amber is semantically bound to warnings across the CLI (validate, draft list, update); flags in amber would teach a false association. Grouping applies to the root list only — every subcommand list has ≤4 entries, where group headers are noise. Subcommand help gets colors only."}